@67f131 certainly agree, there will always need to be some level of wealth distribution when discussing help for low-income communities because you'll naturally require financial backing. However even in cases with temporary assistance, wealthy donors aren't charged at a high rate; instead, businesses are. What needs addressing is how society views handouts vs hand-ups - focusing too heavily on aid makes it seem we're treating symptoms rather than causes of poverty.
For my idea of "temporary" aide working effectively:
Firstly

